From ca205d2a6d73023f555203b8374986b152fee38d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 23:29:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/18] feat(controller): with `continuousZoom` the view
 follows the fingers, and moves once when they lift

Real windows cannot be scaled through Accessibility, so a gesture never touches
them (F16). When it begins the presenter holds the stage; while it lasts the
controller only says how the plane lies: no window is asked anything, nothing is
laid out, drawn, saved or scheduled. When the fingers lift the view moves once,
as `in`, `out`, `zoom`, `desktop`, `show-all` or `neighbor` would move it, and
that move's ordinary motion starts from where the stand-ins are.

So at every moment of a gesture the real windows are where they were when it
began, and whatever takes the stage down leaves things as if it had never begun:
a click, a key, a pause, quitting, new settings, a display that comes or goes,
`hold`. A command or a window that closes goes ahead from where the stand-ins
are; a new window ends the gesture, which would hide it; thirty seconds without
a sign of life end it too.

`stageTransition`, which every change to what a display shows passes, is the one
hook. `forgetFollowing()` goes before every `overlays.finishAnimations()`, and a
test reads the controller's sources for that.

Where nothing can follow (Reduce Motion, the setting off, which is how it ships)
a pinch steps as before, and a pan steps to the neighbours.

A trace holds where a gesture landed and nothing else of it. arbor-sim has
`pinch`, `pan`, `lift`, `click-through` and `reduce-motion`, and draws what the
stage shows while fingers are down. Scenario 41 is a person who never touches
the keyboard.
