+- F16, 2026-10-14: pinching in leads straight to what is under the pointer and pinching out straight to everything, scrubbing through the motion `zoom` and `show-all` would play, instead of scaling evenly about the pointer as the spec's summary says. In a tiled arrangement an even zoom pushes the top and bottom of a full-height window off the display only for the landing to bring them back. The pointer decides what is zoomed in on.
+- F16: not level by level either, which was tried first: in a grid a column grew to three times its width at the same height before anything grew higher. The levels in between are only where the view can land.
+- F16: magnification is taken as a logarithm (0.69 doubles the size), so pinching out by as much as in returns exactly.
+- F16: a pinch under `pinchThreshold` returns to where it began; from there on it always goes somewhere, however far the next level is. Beyond that the level that fits what is in view best wins, and where two fit equally well the direction of the gesture decides.
