+No hosted Mac has a trackpad, so nothing here rests on real fingers. What protects a person is the design: the real windows do not move until the fingers lift, and then they move as a command would move them.
+
+**What a person sees.** With ⌥ held, or on the Desktop, spreading two fingers over a window makes it grow until it fills the view while its neighbours slide off past the edges; pinching
+brings them back. During a pinch two fingers slide the view sideways, up and down; with ⌃⌥ held they slide it sideways without one (up and down they zoom, F55). When the fingers lift, the view settles on a space with the usual motion, from where the fingers left it.
+
+**The virtual camera** (`ZoomGesture`, ArborCore, pure) is a rectangle on the plane as it looks when the gesture begins.
+
+- Pinching in leads straight to the window (tab group, tile, the Desktop) under the pointer, pinching out straight to everything. On the way the view is a blend of the two ends, which is what F02's
+  motion between them shows: a pinch scrubs through the motion of `zoom` or `show-all`. The side that has furthest to go follows the fingers (magnification 0.69 doubles it) and the other arrives at the same moment.
+  **Not a free camera that scales evenly about the pointer**, as the summary above suggests: in a tiled arrangement a window in the left half is already as high as it can get, and an even zoom would push
+  its top and bottom off the display only for the landing to bring them back. The pointer decides *what* is zoomed in on.
+  **Not level by level either** (the first version was): in a grid of three by three, the column first grew to three times its width at the same height, and the pictures of its windows with it. On
+  the straight way a picture is never stretched more than it will be when it has arrived.
+- The **levels** in between, the spaces `out` and `in` would visit, are where the view can land.
+- Past everything the arrangement goes on shrinking, centred, against growing resistance: 84% after 0.3 of magnification (TrellisWM's demo shows about 85%), never under 65%. Past a single window, a tab group or a
+  tile it grows by at most 8%. What goes further than 0.75 past an end is forgotten, so that turning back shows at once.
+- A pan moves the content `panSensitivity` (1.8) points per point the fingers report, at every zoom. The edge of everything resists, and gives at most 12% of the view.
+
+**The landing** (`ZoomGesture.landing`): the pinch picks the level, the pan picks the space.
+
+- The level that best fits what the pinch has brought into view wins (`Navigation.bestFit`'s measure, among the levels); where two fit equally well, the direction of the gesture decides.
+- Less than `pinchThreshold` (0.25) of magnification returns to where the gesture began. That much or more always goes somewhere, however far away the next level is, so a pinch that steps with
+  `continuousZoom` off also moves the view with it on.
+- A pan lands on the space that best fits the level's rectangle, moved as far as the pan went and up to a sixth of the view further: a third of the way to the neighbour is enough. Spaces around or inside
+  the level do not take part: between two windows "both" would fit best, and nobody who slides sideways means to zoom out.
+- The result is **one** `moveCamera`, as `in`, `out`, `zoom-node`, `desktop`, `show-all` or `neighbor` would make: same frames, same focus, one entry in the history.
+  Back to where it began, and a cancelled gesture, touch nothing at all: no window, no history, no save.
+
