+| In the middle of a gesture | What happens |
+|---|---|
+| a command, a tile click, a window closing, minimizing or docking by a rule | it goes ahead, its motion starts from where the stand-ins are, the rest of the gesture is ignored |
+| a command that changes nothing in view (`status`, `save-view`, one that fails, one for another display) | the gesture goes on |
+| a click or a key press | the stage comes down at once, nothing has moved, the rest is ignored |
+| a peek (F51) under way | the pinch's landing is the peek's: one entry in the history, from the view that was left. Letting the keys go first ends the pinch |
+| a new window | the view returns, so that the stage does not hide it |
+| `pause`, quitting, new settings, a display plugged in or unplugged (the last one too), a Space switch, sleep | the stage comes down at once |
+| `hold` (F58) | the view returns, and no gesture is followed while the view is held. Holding still, as during a call, a pinch is asking and is followed |
+| a drag or an edge resize under way when it begins | the gesture is ignored |
+| 30 s without a sign of life | the view returns (fingers that lie still send nothing, so this is only a net under a lost end) |
+
+- The gesture belongs to the display under the pointer when it begins, and is laid out with that display's own options (F25). Other displays notice nothing.
+- While nothing is docked on that display no stage goes up: there is nowhere to go, and Arbor shows nothing of itself (product owner).
+- With a floating window under the pointer a pinch in leads to the Desktop, where it lives (a guest: to the space it opened over), as `zoom` of that window does. Framing what lies beneath would take it out of view under the hand. Windows that stay in view are looked through.
+- **Reduce motion**: there is no stage, so a pinch steps as with `continuousZoom` off, and a pan steps to the neighbours (`PanSteps`): a third of the view for the first, a whole view for each one after, as a view that followed would land.
+- **`animationDuration` 0**: the view still follows the fingers, which is not an animation, and lands at once.
+- `desktopPinchSteps` (T41, default false): a pinch that begins on the bare Desktop steps; an ⌥-pinch over a window still follows.
+
+A gesture that begins while the last landing is still playing lets that motion run out under the fingers: the stand-ins' animations are left alone, and lead to wherever `follow` puts the stand-ins meanwhile.
+
+**The stage comes into sight with the first movement**, and fades in evenly over 160 ms, ten frames (`docs/VIABLE.md`, "Hand-over"). Fingers that only rest change nothing on the display. Only a stage that is held can afford a fade: the real windows under it stand still, so what shows through meanwhile is what the stand-ins start from.
+
+**What F02 got**: two presenter calls, `hold(_ scene:) -> Bool` and `follow(_ frame:) -> Bool`, with about 130 lines in `Stage` and 25 in `StageDirector`; four lines in `stageTransition`; a key press now ends a motion as a click does.
+
+**Traces** hold where a gesture landed (`gestureLanded`) and nothing else of it: until then nothing real changes.
+
+**arbor-sim** (after `set continuousZoom true`): `pinch <amount> [down]`, `pan <dx> <dy> [down]`, `lift [cancelled]`, `click-through`, `reduce-motion on|off`. While the fingers are down it draws what the stage shows.
+
+**Not shipped**
+
+- The default. See "How it is proven".
+- Windows that keep their place on the display (pinned, summoned, left alone) are drawn as stand-ins like the rest. Once the stage can leave holes (F02's second step) they are seen through it, live.
+- ⌃⌥- and ⌃⌥= stay reserved and unbound: through this model a key press is one level, which is what ⌃⌥↑ and ⌃⌥↓ do.
+- What pinching in leads to is decided when the gesture begins. After a pan in the same gesture it is still the window that was under the pointer then; the landing looks at where the view is, so it comes out right.
+- A group's tile is as far in as one gesture goes.
-- 
