From 892739c2e9f7077edc8da8288726763c9bec297b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:32:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/18] fix(gesture): six ways in which a gesture ended
 wrongly, or did not end

From a review of the series. Each was reproduced in arbor-sim and has a test
that failed first.

- A landing moved the view without saying who led. After a stepped pinch the
  next shortcut goes on where the pointer is (F56); after a followed one it went
  back to the keyboard's display. A landing is a gesture's command now.
- With Reduce Motion a slide against the edge of everything counted a step that
  was refused, so the way back took back a step that never happened and ended
  on the neighbour at the other side. A refused step is not counted and not
  asked for again until the view has moved, and of the push as much is
  remembered as a view that followed remembers there.
- `summon` places and parks windows by hand, with no layout, so the stage stayed
  up over them. It ends the gesture. The chaos lab has a rule for the family:
  nothing a held stage shows is moved behind it.
- The stage's own monitors take it down without a word to the controller (a key,
  sleep). A cancelled gesture, the next one, the timer or a new window then
  played a motion back from where the fingers had been, over windows that stood
  at rest in plain sight. Whatever ends a gesture asks first whether the stage
  is still there.
- "Nothing is docked" counted minimized and hidden windows as docked, so a stage
  went up with nowhere to go.
- A window Arbor leaves alone that was minimized or moved kept its stand-in.

The walk of random steps follows less in some seeds now that it has fewer
stages with nowhere to go. Its floor for how much it follows, which is about the
walk and not about Arbor, is half of what the six seeds do today.
